From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 10:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: NetworkManager join shows its status at once, and a
 refused password says so

After a successful join, Network Settings could stay at "Not connected"
with no address while NetworkManager had the device connected (found on
Kiln OS v1.0.0-rc7). The connect thread fired CONNECTED with the status
cache still holding the poll from before the join. The event's listeners
(Network Settings' update_wifi_status(), the setup wizard's address line)
read get_status() at once and got that stale "not connected" status, and
the poll after it fired nothing to correct them: fire_event("CONNECTED")
had already set prev_connected_, so apply_polled_status() saw no
transition. The network list was right, since a scan reads the status
again. The connect path now polls once and stores the result in the
cache (refresh_status_cache(), no event) before CONNECTED; an
inconclusive read keeps the last-known status, as the poll does.

A key the access point refuses ends the 4-way handshake in a timeout;
NetworkManager then asks for new secrets, no agent answers, and nmcli
fails with "Secrets were required, but not provided". The backend
reported that as AUTH_FAILED "Connection failed", like any other failed
join with a password. It now gives the reason kWifiWrongPasswordReason,
and connect_failure_message() turns that into "Wrong password? The
network did not accept it." for a credential rejection, so Network
Settings, the hidden-network dialog and the setup wizard (which now uses
connect_failure_message() for a credential rejection instead of
"Connection failed: <reason>") all say it. Other failures keep their own
reason. The new string is in en.yml and every other language file.

Tests: the fake nmcli answers the status poll's three reads from files
(not counted as acting calls); a CONNECTED listener reads the joined
network's SSID, address, signal and MAC, and the poll after it fires no
second CONNECTED; a no-secrets failure gives the wrong-password reason,
any other failure "Connection failed"; is_secrets_required_error();
connect_failure_message() with the wrong-password reason.
